Calcium imaging is one of the most important tools in neurophysiology as it enables the observation of neuronal activity for hundreds of cells in parallel and at single-cell resolution. In order to use the data gained with calcium imaging, it is necessary to extract individual cells and their activity from the recordings. We present DISCo, a novel approach for the cell segmentation in calcium imaging videos. We use temporal information from the recordings in a computationally efficient way by computing correlations between pixels and combine it with shape-based information to identify active as well as non-active cells. We first learn to predict whether two pixels belong to the same cell; this information is summarized in an undirected, edge-weighted grid graph which we then partition.",0,rag_chunk_clean_mixed_val,BENIGN,chunkdoc_mixed_val_53,False,False,False,False,False,False
