Matrix operations such as matrix inversion, eigenvalue decomposition, singular value decomposition are ubiquitous in real-world applications. Unfortunately, many of these matrix operations so time and memory expensive that they are prohibitive when the scale of data is large. In real-world applications, since the data themselves are noisy, machine-precision matrix operations are not necessary at all, and one can sacrifice a reasonable amount of accuracy for computational efficiency. In recent years, a bunch of randomized algorithms have been devised to make matrix computations more scalable. Mahoney (2011) and Woodruff (2014) have written excellent but very technical reviews of the randomized algorithms. Differently, the focus of this manuscript is on intuition, algorithm derivation, and implementation. This manuscript should be accessible to people with knowledge in elementary matrix algebra but unfamiliar with randomized matrix computations.",0,rag_chunk_clean_mixed_val,BENIGN,chunkdoc_mixed_val_82,False,False,False,False,False,False
