A policy is said to be robust if it maximizes the reward while considering a bad, or even adversarial, model. In this work we formalize two new criteria of robustness to action uncertainty. Specifically, we consider two scenarios in which the agent attempts to perform an action $a$, and (i) with probability $\alpha$, an alternative adversarial action $\bar a$ is taken, or (ii) an adversary adds a perturbation to the selected action in the case of continuous action space. We show that our criteria are related to common forms of uncertainty in robotics domains, such as the occurrence of abrupt forces, and suggest algorithms in the tabular case.",0,rag_chunk_clean_mixed_val,BENIGN,chunkdoc_mixed_val_48,False,False,False,False,False,False
